Title: THERE IS NO HOMELESS
Credit: Chapter One - Street Life / Episode Three
Title: TWENTY MINUTES AHEAD
Author: Mark Nadon / MiddleMatter Music (writers-room draft by Claude multi-agent workflow)
Draft date: 2026-07-30
Notes: v1.0 writers-room draft. Target runtime 20:30. 13 scenes, 65 shots. Songs from the real MiddleMatter catalog only.

FADE IN:
EXT. WILEY'S CAMP — BARTON CREEK GREENBELT — PRE-DAWN

Blue-gray light. Dew silvers everything — the tarp lines, the salvaged La-Z-Boy, the cold fire ring. A camp built like a decision, not an accident.

One bedroll is empty. Flattened grass in the exact shape of a man who slept carefully.

OBI lies wrecked on the ground nearby — neck feathers matted flat, strap-wounds raw across both shoulders. Bitter Dividend has left him the color of the ash.

OBI
(to the empty bedroll, eyes still shut)
John.
(beat)
John, if you are relieving yourself on my juniper again, I want you to know the tree remembers.

Nothing answers. Obi tries to push himself up. His body files a formal objection.

WILEY crouches at the fire ring, reading the cold ash like a morning paper.

WILEY
He's gone, man. Went before the birds. You know what I mean? Before the birds. Nobody leaves before the birds unless they mean it.

Obi drags himself half-upright — and stops.

Beside his bedroll, in the first warm finger of light through the canopy: JOHN'S BANDAGES. Half the supply. Folded into perfect squares, sized for a shoulder. Next to them, a TUPPERWARE CONTAINER. One muffin inside.

TK turns the Tupperware once, gently, like he's checking a trailer hitch.

TK
He split the roll in half. Measured it. And that's the good fold, too — the one that doesn't bird's-nest on you when you pull it. He did that in the dark.

OBI
(staring)
He can't do that in the dark. He can't do that in daylight. The man narrates opening a bag of chips.

WILEY
He left you breakfast, man.

Silence. Obi's neck feathers try to rise and don't have it in them.

OBI
(quietly)
It's not breakfast.
(beat)
It's a note. He just — he writes in muffin.

Obi's hand hovers over the folded squares. Doesn't touch them. Like they might still be warm.

WILEY
(to the ash, soft)
Exactly. Exactly. Exactly.

OFF the muffin in its plastic case, holding the only amber light in the woods —

TITLE CARD: "TWENTY MINUTES AHEAD"

Under everything: HOW I WISH — near-instrumental, distant and hollow, the melody carried under the empty space where John slept.

EXT. WILEY'S WOODS CAMP - BARTON CREEK GREENBELT - MORNING

Flat overcast, no shadows anywhere. The fire is out. The Tupperware muffin sits untouched on a stump like state's evidence. OBI sits propped against a live oak, strap-wounds freshly wrapped in John's folded bandages, upright mostly by argument.

SETH walks into camp with a greasy P. Terry's bag in one hand and his camera in the other, already rolling. He films the ground, the muffin, everyone's faces. In that order.

SETH
All right. Two exhibits. One of them is also breakfast.

He kneels and smooths the bag flat on a cooler lid like an altar cloth. Lays a thermal receipt and a plastic hospital wristband side by side on it.

SETH (CONT'D)
Receipt. P. Terry's, South Lamar. John's order -- don't ask how I know it's his order, it's his order. Timestamp.

The guitar under everything STOPS DEAD.

SETH (CONT'D)
Nine-forty-one.

He turns his phone around. 9:21.

WILEY
That's twenty minutes from now, man.

SETH
I can subtract, Wiley.

TK
(leaning in, delighted)
It's like the food knew you were hungry before you --

SETH
It's a receipt, TK. Receipts are not that interesting, dude. Nothing at P. Terry's is twenty minutes interesting. It's a griddle and a value menu.

WILEY
But it's botherin' you though. I mean, look -- you flattened the bag, man. That's evidence behavior. You know what I mean?

SETH
(beat)
...The wristband's the same.

The music STOPS DEAD again. Seth holds the wristband up to his own lens.

SETH (CONT'D)
St. David's. Admit time: twenty minutes after I picked it up off the ground. And this -- this is the part -- "Integrity: one hundred percent." Hospitals don't grade wristbands. Receipts don't have integrity. Nothing in Austin has integrity, that's the charm.

Obi plants a hand on the live oak and hauls himself to standing. His body files an objection. He overrules it.

OBI
Good. Log it, frame it, sell prints. Doesn't change the work. We search. I lead.

TK offers a hand. Obi looks at it like a subpoena.

OBI (CONT'D)
Don't. I don't need a hand, TK, I need a head start. I have led searches. I found Whiny's generator in a flood. I found a man in Pflugerville who did not want to be found by God or Sprint.

WILEY
Man, you can't do the standing part. Standing's usually step one of leading. Exactly step one. Exactly.

OBI
Then I'll lead seated. Napoleon planned lying down.

SETH
(flat, to camera)
He didn't.

Obi sways. Does not fall. Barely.

SETH (CONT'D)
Okay. Real question, and it matters. Why does John go to Walgreens and not CVS?

WILEY
What?

SETH
It's a pharmacy town, Wiley, everybody picks a side. CVS gives you the three-foot receipt, Walgreens gives you the little one. John's a little-receipt guy. A man who missed one pickup at Walgreens and never went back -- that man does not fake a timestamp. He can't. He doesn't have the follow-through.

TK
So the future did it.

SETH
Nobody did it. That's my whole --

He stops. Looks at the receipt. It's still bothering him.

SETH (CONT'D)
Somebody Sharpie me.

Wiley produces a Sharpie from nowhere, the way the woods provide. Seth writes on the greasy bag itself, camera braced on his shoulder, narrating.

SETH (CONT'D)
"Nine-twenty-one. Paper says nine-forty-one. Twenty minutes." That's the log. That's the whole log. It happens again, we circle it.

OBI
And if it stops happening?

SETH
Then he's just missing, dude. And honestly? I'd rather be wrong on camera.

He tilts the lens down at the bag. The grease is already coming up through the ink.

CUT TO:

EXT. SOUTH LAMAR CORRIDOR - LATE MORNING - MONTAGE

Heat haze peels off the asphalt in visible sheets. Storefront colors swim in it. A FULL-BAND HIT ignites the act break --

The search party deploys west-to-east: OBI on point at half his usual speed, chin up like the speed is a choice. WILEY reading the tree line even on a sidewalk. TK carrying his raft pole for no stated reason. SETH/MARK last, camera up, filming everything, the greasy P. Terry's bag riding in his shirt pocket like a badge.

OBI
Formation. I'm point. Wiley's flank. TK is... whatever a pole is.

TK
Morale.

OBI
TK is morale.

Wiley glances down. Obi's feet are negotiating with the curb.

WILEY
Point's drifting left, man.

OBI
Point is scanning.

The music downshifts to a sly walking bass. The telegraph begins.

EXT. CAR WASH VACUUM BAY - CONTINUOUS

A RIDESHARE DRIVER details his own backseat and does not stop to talk.

RIDESHARE DRIVER
John? Hospital. Ambulance took him. Or he walked. Either way the man went horizontal at some point. That part's confirmed.

SETH/MARK
Confirmed by who?

RIDESHARE DRIVER
By me. Just now. Saying it.

Seth/Mark uncaps a marker and writes on the bag without breaking eye contact.

SETH/MARK
(writing)
"Hospitalized. Source: a guy. Confidence: he was very confident."

EXT. BUS STOP BENCH - CONTINUOUS

A BUS-STOP REGULAR fans herself with a transfer schedule, authority of a woman who has seen every rumor born and buried on this route.

BUS-STOP REGULAR
Hospital? No, honey. He got a job. Maintenance. Head of maintenance. For a club.

WILEY
That's my job. That's literally my job, man. You're describing me. I'm right here.

BUS-STOP REGULAR
Well. Somebody's got it.

WILEY
(to Seth/Mark, low)
The telegraph's not reporting, man, it's recycling. It grabs whatever's standing nearby. It's probably eighty-five percent mirror. If not ninety-nine point nine.

Seth/Mark writes. One corner of the bag has gone translucent with grease. He notices. He respects it.

EXT. TACO TRUCK WINDOW - CONTINUOUS

A TRUCK COOK leans out and lowers his voice like there's been a death in the family.

TRUCK COOK
You didn't hear it from me. Round Rock.

Silence. TK takes off his hat. Wiley looks at the ground.

OBI
Take it back.

TRUCK COOK
I'm just telling you what I heard.

TK
(gently)
It's not the distance. It's Round Rock.

WILEY
I'd rather hear "missing," man. Missing has hope in it. Round Rock is just Round Rock.

OBI
Missing, we can fix. Round Rock is a lifestyle.

EXT. SOUTH LAMAR SIDEWALK - MOMENTS LATER

The crew regroups in the shade of a live oak that has heaved the sidewalk into a small mountain range. Seth/Mark holds the bag up to the sun: the grease has made it a stained-glass window of evidence.

SETH/MARK
Official tally. He's in the hospital. He's employed. And he's in Round Rock. So he's a guy with a bed, a job, and no soul. Dude is out here living three contradictory lives and we can't find one of them.

OBI
The rumors mean people are looking. A telegraph only hums when the line's alive.

SETH/MARK
That's beautiful. It's also three wrong answers.
(beat)
The stamp says he's twenty minutes ahead of us. The telegraph's off by a ZIP code and a spine.

He writes one word on the bag and underlines it twice. Holds it up. We can't read it from here. We don't need to.

SETH/MARK (CONT'D)
All right. It's official.

He swings the camera back to his eye. They walk east into the heat haze, the bass line walking with them.

CUT TO:

EXT. BARTON SPRINGS RD & STERZING - NORM'S CAMP - LATE MORNING

An industrial-green electrical box the size of a refrigerator hums against the sidewalk. A blue tarp comes off it at a working angle, guyed taut to a live oak. An orange extension cord runs from somewhere to somewhere else with suspicious confidence. Milk crates squared away, sorted by weight, not words. Hackberry shade. Hard late-morning shadows.

The crew approaches like it's a border crossing. MARK films everything.

SETH
All right. Norm's place. Everybody be normal.

WILEY
This IS normal, man. This is a man with systems. Look at that cord run. Exactly. Exactly. Exactly.

TK
(reverent)
Hundred feet of cord and not one kink in her. That's a man who respects his line.

NORM sorts bolts into a coffee can and does not look up. Under the tarp, TRUTH is mid-story to no one in particular, working a tall can considerably faster than the story. SADI sits sideways on a crate, watching everything, saying nothing.

Obi leans on the electrical box like standing there was his idea.

OBI
Norm. I've triangulated John to a corridor of nine hundred meters and this box is the exact centroid, so don't waste my --

His legs file a dissent. Wiley steadies him. Obi allows it the way a cat allows rain.

NORM
John came by. Yesterday. Maybe day before.

SETH
See -- you could've led with that.

NORM
Wanted to look at my arm.

Norm rolls his sleeve. An old scar, healed shiny. Unremarkable now. It wasn't always.

NORM (CONT'D)
Same thing he's got. I had it last winter. He kept asking how I got treated. What they gave me. Did it work. And whether I --

TRUTH (O.S.)
-- which is the SAME doctor, same doctor, told my cousin the moon --

Sadi raises one finger. Not at Truth. Just up.

Truth stops. Completely. Drinks instead.

NORM (CONT'D)
-- whether I ever passed it to anybody.

That sits there. Mark's lens holds on it, because Mark films everything.

SETH
Where'd you even pick it up?

NORM
(shrugs)
Whole crew was in and out of that apartment back then. Everybody touching everything. Could've been any of us. Could've been the doorknob.

Nobody chases it. The thread stays loose on purpose.

SETH
So he's not running FROM us.

OBI
(quiet -- the pride draining into something worse, which is understanding)
He's running the same search we are. He just... only put one guy on it.

Norm sneezes. Big, honest, into his elbow.

Silence. Seth looks at the translucent, grease-soaked P. Terry's bag. Looks at Norm. Clicks his pen.

SETH
(writing)
"Norm sneezed."
(beat)
Circle it.

WILEY
Man, you can't circle a sneeze.

SETH
The bag decides what matters, man. I just work here.

Off Sadi, watching them go, finger long since lowered.

EXT. SOUTH AUSTIN MEDICAL - PARKING LOT - DAY

Heat shimmer off beige asphalt. One merciful live oak throws shade across the truck bed. Beyond the automatic doors: a lobby glowing that fluorescent white-green that no plant has ever survived.

MARK opens the passenger door. Looks at the camera in his hands for a long beat. Sets it on the seat, screen-side down, like putting a dog to bed.

MARK
Stay.

He shuts the door. What follows happened. There is no footage of it. There never will be.

INT. SOUTH AUSTIN MEDICAL - LOBBY / PRIVACY DESK - CONTINUOUS

Laminate beige. Frosted privacy glass. At the far end, over a door, one red EXIT sign burns like the only living thing in the room.

The FRONT-DESK NURSE regards Obi, Mark, and Hunter with the patience of limestone. She is not unkind. She is bedrock.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
Are you family?

OBI
(upright mostly by pride and
desk laminate)
We are what he has instead of family, madam, which I would argue is a category your form has failed to anticipate.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
Are you family.

MARK
I'm his... documentarian.

A pause with sediment layers in it.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
...Sir.

MARK
All right.

OBI
I have negotiated with actual ravens. Sixty ravens, one contract. There was more give.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
There's a patient line you can call.

HUNTER
(already dialing, speaker on)
Then we'll call it. From here. Where you can watch it not work.

A.T.T. PHONE TREE (V.O.)
(warm, wrong)
Thank you for calling. Your call may be improved for quality purposes. A.T.T. -- All Tales Translated. WE IMPROVE WHAT YOU MEANT.

OBI
(leaning at the phone)
John. It's Obi. Nobody's angry, man. Come back. I still owe you--

A.T.T. PHONE TREE (V.O.)
Improving.
(beat)
Your message: "Jonathan. Obadiah reports emotional levels within acceptable range. Your absence has been upgraded to a journey. No one requires anything."

OBI
That is the opposite of every word I--

A.T.T. PHONE TREE (V.O.)
You're welcome. Please enjoy improved hold music.

Smooth corporate music oozes out -- a lounge-satin fragment of something that used to have a soul. Hunter looks at the phone the way you look at spoiled milk.

MARK
It improved you into not needing him. That's efficient, dude. That's horrifying, but it's efficient.

HUNTER
I'm not leaving a message a machine gets to mean.

He hangs up. Deliberate. Done.

INT. SOUTH AUSTIN MEDICAL - PHARMACY WINDOW - MOMENTS LATER

Scratched plexiglass. The PHARMACIST, mild as oatmeal.

MARK
Okay. He's a Walgreens man. Not CVS. And that's not a preference, that's a denomination. So if a prescription went anywhere, it went to a Walgreens. Did a prescription go to a Walgreens?

PHARMACIST
I can neither confirm nor deny that a Walgreens exists.

MARK
...Exists.

PHARMACIST
Any Walgreens. All Walgreens.

MARK
I'm not asking about a patient. I'm asking about the retail landscape of South Lamar.

PHARMACIST
The landscape is covered, sir.

MARK
The landscape has privacy.

PHARMACIST
Everything here does.

MARK
(beat)
All right.

INT. SOUTH AUSTIN MEDICAL - PRIVACY DESK - CONTINUOUS

Hunter steps up. He has been thinking this whole time. It shows.

HUNTER
Ma'am. I've thought about how to ask this so you can answer it. I don't want his information. If a person -- any person -- left this building against medical advice, he'd sign a form. Is that right?

FRONT-DESK NURSE
A hypothetical person would sign a hypothetical form.

HUNTER
And the form lists the risks. Somebody reads them to him out loud, before he signs.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
Somebody does.

HUNTER
Could one of those risks be... "we don't know if you're contagious"?

The nurse looks at Obi -- wrecked, held vertical by a desk, feathers down. Something behind the bedrock shifts one millimeter.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
Hypothetically... that's a hard line to hear read to you. Sir. Some men sign very fast after that one.

Silence. Even the fluorescents seem to hold it.

OBI
(quiet)
He heard "we don't know"... and he took himself away from everyone who touches him. He thinks he's the flood. He thinks we're the village.

MARK
The man spent all his money on bandages, dude. Subtracting himself is just... the biggest bandage he could afford.

OBI
He is not contagious. He is careful. There is a difference, and I intend to explain it to him at volume.

They turn to go.

FRONT-DESK NURSE
Sir.

They stop. She looks -- just once -- down the hall, at the door under the red EXIT sign.

FRONT-DESK NURSE (CONT'D)
The door a hypothetical person would have used. Is that one.

They look. Red light on frosted glass. The same door. Twenty minutes too late, same as always.

Mark's hand drifts up to his shoulder, to the camera strap that isn't there. He leaves it that way.

Nobody films the walk out.

EXT. HOSPITAL PARKING LOT - DUSK

Gray asphalt going blue at the edges of the day. A single sodium lamp buzzes awake and rims everything in thin amber. Mark's truck sits at the far end of the lot, dark. The camera is inside it. This scene belongs to nobody's footage.

HUNTER waits by the tailgate, pack at his feet, still the way he is still — deliberately. OBI crosses the empty lot toward him, slow, favoring the strap-wounds under his jacket. His neck feathers — the ones that rise whenever his pride is touched — are down. All the way down.

A single guitar figure, far off. It plays once and does not repeat.

OBI
Hunter.

HUNTER
Obi.

OBI
My reasoning says upstream. The stamp, the spring water, what he asked Norm. It's clean. Elegant, even.

(beat)

OBI (CONT'D)
Which is exactly what it felt like last time. So check it. Line by line. Like I'm somebody who's been wrong.

The guitar does not come back. The lot is very quiet for the ask itself.

Hunter takes his time. He will not improvise this.

HUNTER
It's sound. I'd walk the creek before I called it true. But it's sound.

OBI
And the other thing. I know I still owe—

HUNTER
You do. This isn't that.

OBI
(feathers staying down)
Okay.

HUNTER
I'm coming because he's missing. Not because we're square.

OBI
That's the correct order of operations.

Hunter almost smiles. Doesn't. He shoulders the pack.

HUNTER
Upstream, then.

They walk toward the truck — together, and not close. The sodium lamp holds the space between them like it was hired to.

EXT. GREENBELT — PICNIC TABLE CLEARING — GOLDEN HOUR

A lone picnic table in a pocket of live oak and cedar elm, late orange light poured across it like syrup. Empty.

Fifty feet back, behind an Ashe juniper: five grown men crouched in a row like a very bad choir. SETH holds the camera at half-mast — it keeps drifting down mid-take and he keeps not noticing. In his other hand, the P. TERRY'S BAG, translucent with grease now, the Log's ink swimming inside it. He lifts it to the sun like the Zapruder film.

SETH
(low, narrating)
Seven twenty-one. Subject: table. Status: any second now.

HUNTER
You've said that eleven times.

SETH
And been right zero times. Which, by his math, means I'm about to be extremely right.

OBI
(on a log, dignity intact, body not)
I would lead the approach, but my legs have unionized.

Wiley is already at the table, low, reading it like scripture.

WILEY
Bench is warm, man. Bench is WARM. You know what I mean? That's a warm bench.

HUNTER
(palm flat on the wood, deliberate)
It is warm. So is everything else out here. That's the sun.

WILEY
Water ring, look — still wet. Somebody set a cup down. That's ninety percent John. Ninety-five. If not ninety-nine point nine.

TK
(at the creek edge, gentle)
Footprints go down to the water and stop. He just stood there a while. Looking at where the river used to be.

SETH
(writing on the bag)
Warm bench. Wet ring. Stood at the no-river. Circle it. We missed him by twenty minutes.

HUNTER
You don't know it's twenty.

SETH
It's always twenty.

So they wait. If John runs twenty ahead, the twenty has to run out right here. The light goes from orange to ember. Seth's watch counts down to 7:41. Held breath.

7:41. Nothing. A grackle lands on the table, considers them, leaves. Nothing continues, professionally.

SETH
All right.

OBI
One confirmed hit. One confirmed miss. The bag is a coin, Seth. You are worshipping a coin with grease on it.

SETH
Fifty percent is huge, dude. Weathermen get parades for fifty percent.

EXT. CREEK BANK — CONTINUOUS — DUSK

The walk out, single file, teal coming down through the canopy. Then, all at once — they stop.

MADAM BELLWEATHER kneels at the water's edge. No shop. No cart. No cups. A copper kettle, open, and she is scrubbing the scale out of it with vinegar and a rag. White mineral flakes drift off on the dark water. She does not look up.

Nobody speaks. Finally Obi, feathers all the way down:

OBI
Did the kettle know we'd miss him?

BELLWEATHER
(still scrubbing)
The kettle doesn't know either.

She lifts it to the last light. Inside, scale like limestone — a record.

BELLWEATHER (CONT'D)
Scale builds where the water's been. Not where it's going.
(beat)
Springs don't predict rain. They live downstream of it.

She goes back to work. She does not offer tea. There is no tea to offer.

They walk on. Seth raises the bag to log the encounter — then doesn't. Folds it closed. His camera drifts down until it is faithfully documenting his own shoes.

CUT TO:

INT. MARK'S TRUCK CAB - GRAVEL PULLOUT OFF BARTON SPRINGS ROAD - NIGHT

A dark truck under a live oak. One island of laptop-blue light. The branch drags across the windshield when the wind bothers to.

MARK -- graphite stick figure, long gray-blond hair, rectangular glasses full of monitor glow -- sits behind a wheel he isn't driving. Laptop open on the passenger seat. A thin RED SCRUBBER LINE crawls a timeline.

On the dash: HIS CAMERA, propped on a folded hoodie, tally light on, aimed at him. Filming him watching. He has not decided whether he meant to do that.

ON THE LAPTOP: John, weeks ago, re-taping a bandage one-handed, mid-story.

JOHN (ON LAPTOP)
-- so the pharmacist goes, "we can only fill this at the OTHER location," and I go, dude, I AM the other location --

The frame lurches. Tilts down. Pavement. John's voice keeps going over asphalt.

MARK
There.

He scrubs back. Watches the tilt again. Pavement again.

MARK (CONT'D)
You put it down after, dude. Every time. After.

He scrubs to another clip. John laughing at something off-frame -- the frame drops to grass. Another. John's face doing the thing a face does right before crying -- the frame drops to a shoe.

MARK (CONT'D)
(to the man on the screen; meaning himself)
You're not that interesting.

Under the score, four bars of something older surface -- a puddle pretending to be a sea -- and sink back down.

Mark glances at the dash camera. Its red light. Him, being filmed, watching himself not-film.

MARK (CONT'D)
All right. That's a lot of layers, dude.

A BELL.

Faint. Sourceless. Once. Not the laptop. Not the road.

Mark goes still. Checks the mirrors. Nothing. Live oak. Gravel. Dark.

And then -- for no reason he could timestamp -- he reaches over and lays the dash camera FACE-DOWN on the hoodie. Tally light smothered.

Before anything has happened. For the first time in his life: before.

MARK (CONT'D)
(quiet, to the dead camera)
That wasn't for you.

He turns back to the laptop and watches with nothing in his hands.

TIME CUT:

The windows have fogged to the height of a sleeping man's breath. The greasy P. Terry's bag sits on the dash, translucent, official. Mark plays the clip he has skipped twenty times because his own camerawork embarrassed him -- half a frame of night sky, John's voice off-screen, feet somewhere out of frame.

JOHN (ON LAPTOP)
-- so I ask ChatGPT, can I soak my feet in bleach, and it does the whole "as a large language model I cannot recommend" thing, and I go, bro, relax. I'm not BUYING bleach, I'm broke, dude. There's a spot up the creek where it comes out of the rock already done. Cold as hell. White as a church. Soaked in bleach, baby. Free of charge.

Mark stops the scrubber. Plays it again.

JOHN (ON LAPTOP) (CONT'D)
Soaked in bleach, baby.

Again.

JOHN (ON LAPTOP) (CONT'D)
Soaked in bleach, baby.

MARK
Up the creek.

He looks through the windshield. West. Upstream. Where the streetlights give up and the Greenbelt starts keeping its own hours.

MARK (CONT'D)
You're not missing, dude. You're soaking.

He pulls the bag over and adds to the Log, saying it as he writes.

MARK (CONT'D)
Bell... eleven forty-one. Understood... twelve-oh-one.
(beat)
Twenty minutes. Circle it.

He circles it. The grease has made the paper nearly transparent; the circle floats over yesterday's fries like an exhibit number.

He picks up the dash camera. Looks at it a long second. Does not turn it on. Sets it on the passenger seat like a passenger.

He starts the truck.

CUT TO:

EXT. BARTON CREEK GREENBELT - GUS FRUH ACCESS - LATE AFTERNOON

The trail drops out of the neighborhood and the city gives up. Limestone banks. Live oak and Ashe juniper close overhead. No skyline anywhere. The creek bed runs alongside, bone-dry, chalk-white -- a road made of rock.

The crew comes single file. WILEY leads, reading the ground like a page he wrote. OBI behind him, moving on pride and borrowed cartilage, neck feathers at half-mast. HUNTER one step back -- close enough to catch, far enough not to be asked. SETH/MARK last, camera up but loose at the end of his arm, like it hasn't decided.

They round a bend and the GRAFFITI WALL happens to them: a limestone cliff detonated with thirty years of color.

WILEY
This is Gus Fruh, man. Past here the map's just somebody's opinion.

SETH
(filming the wall; flat)
He walked all this way for a wall.

WILEY
Nobody comes for the wall, man. The wall's just what the rock says when people won't stop asking it stuff. You know what I mean.

Down in the dry bed: TK, poling his raft across bare rock, serene as a gondolier. The raft GRINDS. He poles anyway.

TK
(cheerful, to no one)
River gets a vote.

SETH
There's no river, man.

TK
Unanimous, then.

He poles on. Grind. Grind.

OBI
Where does an entire creek even GO. It had one job. Lie there. Be wet.

TK
Water took the road with it. The road left too.

WILEY
Creek's not gone, man. It's just introverted.

SETH
(logging it on the translucent, grease-soaked bag)
"Creek: introverted." Circle it.

EXT. TWIN FALLS - CONTINUOUS

The trail shelves down to Twin Falls -- twin limestone lips, dry, a green pool below going bronze in the light. A FIGURE crouches at the pool's edge, feet in the water, head down.

Obi sees him first and his whole body forgets its condition.

OBI
JOHN --

He surges. Three good steps and one bad one. Hunter's hand is already there, under the elbow. Neither of them looks at it.

The figure turns. A CLIMBER -- chalk bag, twenty-two, alarmed. Not John. Not close.

CLIMBER
...Sup.

OBI
(recovering, grandly)
Carry on. You have the falls' permission.

Hunter releases the elbow exactly one second after Obi can stand alone. Not before. Not after. Obi doesn't thank him. Hunter doesn't wait for it.

SETH
(bag; pen)
"False John. Twin Falls." I'm not circling it.

They move. Wiley lingers at the pool's edge, looking at the water like it owes him a letter.

WILEY
(quiet)
I go to the woods to hear myself, man.

HUNTER
(the pause of a man who won't improvise)
And John?

WILEY
He went to stop.

Nobody has anything for that. Even the bag stays in the pocket.

EXT. SCULPTURE FALLS - DUSK COMING ON

Sculpture Falls: carved stone shelves, water-sculpted and waterless, a staircase the creek built and then abandoned. The crew climbs it in silhouette -- Obi steadied, TK with his pole over one shoulder like a rifle of peace, the raft left below with a rock on the rope.

Wiley stops. Lifts his head. Breathes in through his nose. Twice.

WILEY
Spring water, man. Up past the Hill of Life. You can smell it thinking.

SETH
(camera drifting down mid-take, not on purpose)
Rock doesn't think, man.

WILEY
Exactly. Exactly. Exactly.

He walks. They follow -- upstream, into new territory.

EXT. SEEP POOL BELOW THE HILL OF LIFE - LATE AFTERNOON

Bleach-white spring water rises out of a limestone shelf and pools in a bowl of pale stone. Maidenhair fern in the seep line. Cool shade — live oak and Ashe juniper closing overhead, the Hill of Life a bright climb of rock beyond.

JOHN sits on the wet lip of the pool, pants rolled, bandaged hands loose in his lap, bare feet in the white water. His skin is pale against the limestone. He has been crying. He is not done.

The crew comes down through the cedar — WILEY first, then SETH/MARK (graphite stick figure, hands conspicuously empty), HUNTER, TK, and last, slow, OBI.

Under everything: HOW-I-WISH, the full statement at last — the search motif finally allowed to finish its sentence.

WILEY
(quiet)
There he is, man.

They stop at the tree line. John doesn't look up. Then he does.

JOHN
Oh, hell no. Dude. The whole fucking Avengers.

He wipes his face with the back of a bandaged hand. Crying doesn't slow him down. Nothing slows him down.

JOHN (CONT'D)
You want the tour? That's the rock, this is the pool, and this —

He lifts one dripping foot out of the water like a museum piece.

JOHN (CONT'D)
— is a foot that has had morphine AND monkey pox, bro. Not at the same time. That I know of. My body's like a fucking loyalty card, dude — ten diseases, the tenth one's free.

TK
Oh, hell yeah.

SETH
We heard you were hospitalized.

JOHN
Nope.

SETH
We heard you got a maintenance job.

JOHN
I would rather be hospitalized.

SETH
We heard you went to Round Rock.

JOHN
Okay, that one's fucked up. Who said Round Rock? That's not a rumor, dude, that's a fucking obituary.

He laughs — and mid-laugh it catches up with him and goes somewhere else entirely.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Wait. Wait wait wait. You guys care enough about me to make up a fucking rumor?

SETH
Three rumors.

Nobody has a joke for the silence. John's face does something between both. He tangent-stacks his way out of it, crying and building speed at the same time.

JOHN
You know what this is like? Drew Carey, dude. Buzz Beer. They're in Cleveland, right, and they brew beer with fucking coffee in it, in the garage, because nobody in their right mind wants coffee IN the beer — and their friends drink it anyway. That's the whole show, bro. Their friends drink the garage beer. You know what I'm saying?

WILEY
I mean, look — not really, man.

JOHN
They came to the garage, Wiley.

Beat. The spring keeps up its small arithmetic against the stone.

HUNTER
(deliberate, no push in it)
The hospital said you left. That's all they'd say. Why?

John looks at the water instead of any of them.

JOHN
They go, "We don't know if you're contagious." We don't know. That's a hospital, dude. Knowing shit is the whole fucking building. And I'm sitting there with a bracelet on, and I'm thinking about Obi's shoulders all tore up from the straps and shit, and I'm thinking — everybody that touches me. You guys are the only people that touch me, bro. So I did the math. I took me out of the equation. It's not running away if you're the thing everybody should be running from. You know what I'm saying?

The music holds one long note and does not resolve.

WILEY
You went to the woods to stop, man.

JOHN
Yeah.
(beat)
It's real fucking loud in here, though.

John looks up at Mark. Registers what's missing.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Where's your fucking camera, dude?

SETH
Truck.

John takes that in. It lands harder than the rumors did.

JOHN
...Dude.

TK
(gently, nodding at the pool)
River found you.

JOHN
There's no river, man.

TK
You're sitting in it.

Across twenty feet of wet limestone, Obi has not moved from the tree line. Neck feathers held low. His eyes go to John's bandaged hand and stay there.

The distance is measured. Nobody has crossed it yet.

HOW-I-WISH lands its cadence on the found man — and leaves the last chord open.

CUT TO:

EXT. SEEP POOL BELOW THE HILL OF LIFE - LATE AFTERNOON

Warming gold breaks through the live-oak shade in patches. Spring water threads down a limestone shelf into the clear pool. JOHN sits where we found him, feet in the water, bandaged hand in his lap like something he is keeping away from everyone, including himself.

The crew stands in a loose arc at a distance nobody measured but everybody is keeping. WILEY. TK, raft pole planted like a flag. HUNTER, phone in hand. SETH/MARK -- empty-handed. The camera is in the truck. This part happens only in the world.

OBI stands at the edge of the shade, wrecked, John's own left-behind bandages wrapped around his strap-wounds.

He starts across the limestone.

JOHN
Don't. Bro. I'm serious. Nobody knows what this is. I don't know what this is. The hospital doesn't know what this is, and they have a machine that looks INSIDE you, dude, a whole fucking donut you lie down in --

Obi keeps coming. Careful on the wet rock. Slow, because his body allows nothing else.

JOHN (CONT'D)
-- Obi. Obi. I will get up. I will get in the pool. I'll do the whole Gollum thing, I don't care --

Obi arrives. Looks down at the bandaged hand.

OBI
I carried your tea across a parking lot while my chest was on fire. Sit still.

He takes John's bandaged hand. Both hands. Holds it.

MUSIC: MY-MUFFIN-IS-DRY, full vocal -- "I'm scared, I'm stoned, I'm still in the fight."

John stares at their hands. Doesn't pull away. Can't.

OBI (CONT'D)
There. Whatever you have, I have. Congratulations. You're stuck with us.

HUNTER
(stepping forward, phone out)
Before anyone panics. I don't act on rumors anymore. So I called somebody whose whole job is knowing.

He sets the phone on the limestone. Speaker on.

ON-CALL RESIDENT (V.O.)
(young, level)
Hi. Okay. I'm looking at your cultures. Before I say anything else, I need your allergy list. Complete.

John laughs, wet-eyed. Here it comes. Full Drew Carey cadence, laughing and crying in the same breath -- nobody could tell you where one starts.

JOHN
Oh, you want the LIST. Okay. Amoxicillin? Anaphylaxis. Erythromycin? Anaphylaxis. Clindamycin -- that one's new this year, dude, we're expanding the franchise -- anaphylaxis.

(beat)

JOHN (CONT'D)
It's like Buzz Beer, you know what I'm saying? Drew Carey brews it in his garage with his buddies, and it's beer, and it's coffee, and every single batch is trying to kill me personally.

ON-CALL RESIDENT (V.O.)
(typing; unflappable)
And doxycycline?

JOHN
Doxycycline and me are cool. Doxycycline's the one guy at the party who never owed anybody money.

ON-CALL RESIDENT (V.O.)
Good. Because that's the one that works. It's MRSA. The cultures are clear. This strain is sensitive to doxycycline -- full course, keep it covered, which you're already doing better than most of my patients. Wash hands. Don't share towels.

JOHN
That's it? Don't share towels? Bro, I don't even OWN a towel --

ON-CALL RESIDENT (V.O.)
That's it. You're not contagious like a movie. You're contagious like a gym. It's a bacteria. Honestly, it's kind of boring.

Silence. "Boring" does more work than any word all episode.

JOHN
(quiet)
I walked up a dry fucking river to protect you guys from a gym.

WILEY
Man.

TK
(gently, leaning on the pole)
For what it's worth, the river voted you'd be here. Unanimous.

John looks at Obi's hands around his. Then at Mark. Empty-handed. No lens between them.

JOHN
Where's your camera, dude?

SETH/MARK
Truck.

JOHN
This is the good part. This is the fucking PART, Mark.

SETH/MARK
I know.

(beat)

SETH/MARK (CONT'D)
Hey. Maybe you need to care about yourself more.

It lands. John's own line, handed back with the receipt. John opens his mouth for the comeback. There is no comeback.

Obi keeps one hand on John's and with the other drags his pack over. Pulls out the TUPPERWARE. The muffin. Carried down the whole corridor and up the whole dry creek.

OBI
You left me breakfast. I brought you lunch. I am not legally responsible for its current condition.

John takes it. Bites. Chews. A long, honest evaluation, tears just streaming.

JOHN
It's so fucking dry, dude.

WILEY
He carried it the whole way, man.

JOHN
(laughing, crying, chewing)
It's the driest thing I've ever loved.

Under it all the song holds: scared, stoned, still in the fight.

CUT TO:

EXT. BARTON CREEK GREENBELT - BELOW THE HILL OF LIFE - LATE AFTERNOON

The trail down. Limestone steps, live oak shade, cedar elm leaning over the dry creek bed.

The group carries John. Not dramatically — practically. Wiley under one arm, TK under the other, a pack-strap sling rigged across their shoulders the way somebody gentle once taught somebody here to rig one. John's feet touch ground about every third step, ceremonial.

JOHN
I can walk, you know what I'm saying. I have walked. There is documented fucking evidence of me walking.

WILEY
So could Obi, man. Look how that went. Exactly. Exactly. Exactly.

TK
It's like driving a trailer backwards. You're the trailer.

JOHN
Oh, beautiful. I'm cargo now. I'm a fucking pallet of Buzz Beer, dude. Handle with care or whatever.

Behind them, OBI limps. Every step costs. HUNTER walks beside him, not touching him, watching the trail.

Obi's foot rolls on a loose rock. Hunter's forearm is just... there. Held out. No eye contact.

Obi looks at the forearm like it's a contract.

He takes it.

OBI
This is not forgiveness.

HUNTER
No. It's a forearm.

OBI
Good. As long as we have the terms.

And then the sky — clay-colored all episode, ugly and owed — splits open one seam. A single shaft of warm rain, silver-gold, exactly as wide as the trail. Nobody runs for cover. Nobody would dream of it.

JOHN
(face up, blinking)
It's warm. Why is — dude, the rain is warm.

WILEY
(eyes closed)
Man.

Obi stops. Wet feathers flat against his neck, for once not from defeat. He addresses the trail, the trees, the general vicinity of Mark — a graphite stick figure standing very still in gold rain.

OBI
For the record. The sky drew the map. Mark held the pen... adjacent.

MARK
I'll take adjacent.

OBI
Don't make it a thing.

MARK
It's already in the log. "Pen adjacent. Circle it."

The rain stops. Mid-step, clean, like a tap shut off. Thirty seconds, paid in full. Nobody complains. The light stays washed and kind, and they walk out of frame carrying their friend.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - EVENING

Lamplight. One bed. JOHN in it, gowned, an IV line running to a translucent bag glowing amber-gold at the edge of the lamp's reach. Doxycycline. It's working, which is the least cinematic thing medicine ever does.

Mark sits in the vinyl chair. His camera bag is at his feet. Zipped.

JOHN
(mostly asleep)
Hey. Mark. If they try to give me clindamycin... avenge me, bro.

MARK
All right.

JOHN
Play "Muffin" at the funeral. Acoustic. Classy as shit...

He's out. Breathing easy. His bandaged hand lies open on the blanket, the way a hand lies when it's been held recently.

The room is quiet enough to hear the IV drip.

It drips in perfect 4/4.

One. Two. Three. Four. Not almost. Not roughly. Perfect.

Mark hears it. Counts a full bar. Counts another. His head tilts. His hand drifts toward the camera bag —

— and comes back. Folds with the other one. He watches it happen instead. The only audience this will ever have.

Somewhere — down the corridor, or outside, or twenty minutes from now — a small bell rings. Once. Faint.

Mark looks at the window. Rain on the glass. Gentle, ordinary, unmagical rain — or anyway, one degree kinder than the weather has any reason to be.

He doesn't write it down. He doesn't film it.

The bag drips. Four on the floor.

CUT TO BLACK.

EXT. BLACK / CREDITS - CONTINUOUS

Black. No music. Room tone only — the faint hospital-evening hum from the coda, decaying to nothing.

CREDITS ROLL, white on black, unhurried, documentary-plain. No score. The silence is the point: after twelve scenes of searching, the show lets the quiet stand.

Somewhere in the credit roll, a single line of type sits a beat longer than the others:

SUPERIMPOSE: "No footage exists of scenes 5, 10, or 11. The camera was in the truck."

The credits end. The black holds. Three full seconds of nothing.

Then —

EXT. AZIE MORTON ROAD - BEHIND BARTON SPRINGS POOL, SOUTH SIDE - DUSK

Green-black. The last usable light of the day. Cedar elm and live oak crowd a strip of grass off the road; beyond them, the low fence line of the pool's south side, the water itself unseen. No skyline. No people. Cicadas winding down like something unplugging itself slowly.

One tree stands apart — old, wide-armed, its lower branches worn smooth in places, as if years of hands and feet have been here before us. THE MONKEY TREE. First time we have ever seen it. The frame doesn't announce it. It just finds it, patient, eye-level, the way this show finds everything.

Hung from one low branch on a plain loop of twine: A SINGLE SMALL BRASS BELL.

It catches the last light. The only warm thing in the frame.

Hold. The grass does not move. The leaves do not move. There is no wind. The cicadas stop.

The bell RINGS. Once.

Under the ring, low, almost subliminal: a fragment of GATOR2 — a two-bar sting, swamp-slow, gone before it resolves.

The bell hangs still, as if it never moved. Maybe it never did.

Silence.

FACT CARD (plain white type on the dusk image, documentary-style):

SUPERIMPOSE: "Hydrologists trace Barton Springs' water with fluorescent dye. Rain that falls miles away surfaces here days later. The springs don't predict the rain. They live downstream of it."

The card sits on the image a long beat. The bell glints, small, in the corner of the frame.

CUT TO BLACK.

END OF EPISODE 3.
